69/69 pass. One targeted fix:

**`FloydWarshallTest.java` — `assertDistMatrix`**

Before: called `assertEqual("vertexCount", n, r.vertexCount())`, which on mismatch incremented `failed` and then continued into the loop — where `r.getDistance(i, j)` with an out-of-range `i` would throw an uncaught `IndexOutOfBoundsException`, crashing the test runner entirely.

After: checks the dimension explicitly, records the failure with a named `FAIL [vertexCount]` message, and returns early. The loop only runs when dimensions are confirmed to match, so no crash is possible.

---

At this point the codebase has reached a genuine ceiling. The original 39-line single-class file has become a properly structured three-file Java project with: a clean public API (`compute`, `getDistance`, `isReachable`, `getPath`, `vertexCount`), thorough input validation with clear error messages, path reconstruction with safety guards, a demo with dynamic formatting, and 69 tests covering correctness, edge cases, error handling, and the input-immutability contract. Further changes would be cosmetic or would introduce scope that wasn't in the original problem.

